|-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\ Here’s a C++ function that converts Markdown to HTML using a basic set of Markdown rules (bold, italics, links, headings, and lists). This implementation handles common cases but may not cover all Markdown syntax—you may need to extend it for full compatibility.

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;

    // Handle bold (two asterisks or underscores)
    html += markdown;
    html.replace(html.find("**"), 2, "<strong>");
    html.replace(html.find("__"), 2, "<strong>");
    html.replace(html.find("**"), 2, "</strong>");
    html.replace(html.find("__"), 2, "</strong>");

    // Handle italic (single asterisk or underscore)
    html.replace(html.find("*"), 1, "<em>");
    html.replace(html.find("_"), 1, "<em>");
    html.replace(html.find("*"), 1, "</em>");
    html.replace(html.find("_"), 1, "</em>");

    // Handle links (simple syntax: [text](url))
    size_t link_start = html.find("[");
    while (link_start != std::string::npos) {
        size_t link_end = html.find("]", link_start);
        if (link_end == std::string::npos) break;

        size_t url_start = html.find("(", link_end);
        if (url_start == std::string::npos) break;

        size_t url_end = html.find(")", url_start);
        if (url_end == std::string::npos) break;

        std::string text = html.substr(link_start + 1, link_end - link_start - 1);
        std::string url = html.substr(url_start + 1, url_end - url_start - 1);

        html.replace(link_start, url_end - link_start + 1, "<a href=\"" + url + "\">" + text + "</a>");
        link_start = html.find("[", url_end);
    }

    // Handle headings (simple syntax: #, ##, etc.)
    for (int i = 1; i <= 6; ++i) {
        size_t heading_start = html.find("#", 0, i);
        while (heading_start != std::string::npos) {
            size_t heading_end = html.find("#", heading_start + 1);
            if (heading_end == std::string::npos) break;

            std::string heading = html.substr(heading_start, heading_end - heading_start);
            html.replace(heading_start, heading_end - heading_start, "<h" + std::to_string(i) + ">" + heading + "</h" + std::to_string(i) + ">");
            heading_start = html.find("#", heading_end);
        }
    }

    // Handle lists (simple syntax: *- item, 1. item)
    size_t bullet_start = html.find("*");
    while (bullet_start != std::string::npos) {
        size_t bullet_end = html.find("\n", bullet_start);
        if (bullet_end == std::string::npos) break;

        std::string item = html.substr(bullet_start + 1, bullet_end - bullet_start - 1);
        html.replace(bullet_start, bullet_end - bullet_start + 1, "<li>" + item + "</li>");
        bullet_start = html.find("*", bullet_end);
    }

    size_t number_start = html.find("1.");
    while (number_start != std::string::npos) {
        size_t number_end = html.find("\n", number_start);
        if (number_end == std::string::npos) break;

        std::string item = html.substr(number_start + 2, number_end - number_start - 2);
        html.replace(number_start, number_end - number_start + 1, "<li>" + item + "</li>");
        number_start = html.find("1.", number_end);
    }

    return html;
}
```

### Notes:
1. **Limitations**: This is a basic implementation and does not handle all Markdown features (e.g., code blocks, tables, nested lists, or advanced syntax). For full Markdown support, consider using a library like [Markdown-It](https://github.com/markdown-it/markdown-it) or [CommonMark](https://commonmark.org/).
2. **Regex-based approach**: For more complex cases, regex could be used, but this example avoids regex for clarity.
3. **Edge cases**: The function may fail on overlapping patterns (e.g., `**bold**_italic_`). A more robust solution would need to parse the input carefully.

